    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d351">

                <pb facs="0363" n="339" xml:id="pg339"/>

                <head>Mr. Wood to Mr.
                        Seward.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 154.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States</hi>,
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1864-04-02"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >April</hi> 2, 1864</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi>The Austro-Prussian armies have been
                    repulsed in their attacks on Dübbel and Frederica. Before the former place the
                    Prussian army must have lost heavily. His Majesty returned yesterday from the
                    army, pronouncing everything as satisfactory as possible. The Danish government,
                    after declining the first invitation to the English conference, have finally
                    accepted it on the basis of the treaty of 1851-‘52, <hi rendition="#italic">or
                        on some other condition equally favorable</hi>. Prussia will insist on the
                    virtual dismemberment of Denmark; and England, to please her German sovereign,
                    to avoid a war and to preserve her material interest, will consent to it. I am
                    glad to see that this Danish-German question is beginning to be understood in
                    the United States.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to remain, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>BRADFORD R. WOOD, <hi rendition="#italic">Minister
                        Resident.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
